      "role": "user",
      "content": "Wait, what are you talking about with the goblin stuff? lol. I'm looking for actual DevOps advice here. \n\nI've already checked the CPU/Memory and they seem fine during the runs. I'm thinking it might be a race condition or maybe some network flake between the build agent and the database. How can I get better logs or trace the exact moment things go sideways in the container to see if it's a timing issue?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Apologies for the confusion. Let's get down to the nitty-gritty of your Jenkins pipeline issue. If you're suspecting a race condition or network flakiness, then you're on the right track. To get better logs, you can increase the verbosity of your test output. Most testing frameworks allow you to set the log level to a more detailed output, which can help you pinpoint where the tests are failing.\n\nFor tracing timing issues, you can use tools like `tox` or `toxiproxy` to simulate network conditions and see how your tests behave under different scenarios. This can help you identify if network latency or unreliability is causing the flaky tests.\n\nAdditionally, consider using a distributed tracing system like Jaeger or Zipkin. These tools can help you visualize the flow of requests through your system and identify where delays or failures are occurring. By instrumenting your tests to send traces to these systems, you can get a clearer picture of what's happening during the build process.\n\nLastly, if you're using Docker containers, make sure to log the container's output to your Jenkins logs. You can do this by mounting a volume that captures the container's stdout and stderr. This way, you can review the logs even if the container exits unexpectedly.",
      "source": "target",
