      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks, the distributed tracing idea sounds useful. i'll look into jaeger. \n\none more thing though—the failure logs are sometimes totally empty or just cut off mid-sentence right when the crash happens. it's almost like the process just disappears. do you think that could be an OOM killer issue, or is there something else that would cause a process to just vanish without a trace?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Ah, the vanishing act of processes, a classic mystery indeed. An OOM (Out of Memory) killer could certainly be the culprit behind your process disappearing without a trace. When the system runs low on memory, the OOM killer will terminate processes to free up resources, and it might not always log the reason for termination, especially if it's a quick kill.\n\nTo investigate this, you can check the system logs for any OOM killer messages. On Linux systems, you can look at `/var/log/syslog` or use the `dmesg` command to see kernel messages. If you find evidence of an OOM kill, you might need to optimize your Jenkins pipeline to use less memory or increase the available memory on your Jenkins server.\n\nAnother possibility is that your tests are running out of file descriptors, which can also cause processes to fail silently. You can check the number of open file descriptors with the `ulimit -n` command and compare it to the number of files your tests are trying to access.\n\nIf neither of these seems to be the issue, you might want to look into the Jenkins plugin or script that's running the tests. It's possible that there's a bug or a misconfiguration causing the process to terminate unexpectedly. Make",
